<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e8f0f3;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Importance of Water – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #333; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Importance of Water</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How We Use Water Everyday</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Summarize a short story or text. Interactive: Participate in collaborative story-writing with peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can summarize the main points of a text on water importance (e.g., "Water is essential for people's health and plants because it helps them grow and stay alive.")<br>Can contribute sentences in a collaborative story about water conservation (e.g., "The family saved water every day.")<br>Can collaborate in a group to write ideas on water usage and conservation (e.g., "We use water for cooking and cleaning.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students watch a short video (1 minute) showing various ways people use water daily (e.g., showering, cooking, washing clothes, watering plants). After the video, students quickly jot down 3-5 sentences about how their family uses water every day. Teacher asks students to share one sentence orally to refresh memory.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> Teacher writes a simple sentence on the board: "We use water for drinking and cooking." Then adds, "If we waste water, we will have less for everyone." Teacher models the pronunciation of "water" and "important" (focusing on syllabification and stress). Teacher then demonstrates how to add detail: "We use water daily for drinking, cooking, and cleaning because it is an essential resource."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b><br>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the purpose of 'for + -ing' in 'for drinking'?" (To show why we use water.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What happens if we waste water?" (There will be a shortage/less water.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Importance of Water".</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informative text (approx. 80-100 words) titled "Our Water, Our Future". The text describes the importance of water, daily uses, consequences of wasting it, and simple conservation tips. It incorporates vocabulary like <i>essential, potable, shortage, conserve, turn off, leak, daily, because, resource</i>, and grammatical structures such as "We use water for sanitation" and "If we don't save water, we will face a drought."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand the main points in a short, simple text on familiar everyday topics related to their immediate environment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text individually. Then, in pairs, they answer:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main idea of this text?" (Students write their answer).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match two sentences from the text, one expressing a cause and one expressing an effect (e.g., "We need water for plants." → "Plants grow well.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Water is a limited resource." (T/F) "If we turn off the tap, we waste water." (T/F) Students write T or F for two statements.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing clear sentences using First Conditional, "for + verb + ing" to express purpose, and "Subject + verb + complement + because + cause". Use vocabulary: <i>save, waste, shortage, essential, conserve, drink, cook, clean, filter, tap, daily, limited resource</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Teacher provides sentence beginnings and students complete them using the target grammar and vocabulary.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We use water for..." (students complete with "-ing verbs like 'drinking, cooking, cleaning, showering').</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If we waste water, we..." (students complete with 'will face shortages', 'will not have enough for plants').</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We save water because..." (students complete with 'it is a limited resource', 'it is important for life').</li>
                            </ul>
                            Students write 3-4 complete sentences individually.
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Expansion):</b> Students are given simple statements and asked to expand them into more complex sentences using the target structures.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Water is important." (Students expand to: "Water is important for our health because it keeps us alive.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Turn off the tap." (Students expand to: "If you turn off the tap, you will save water for everyone.")</li>
                            </ul>
                            Students write 2-3 expanded sentences individually.
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> Students exchange their completed and expanded sentences with a partner. They read each other's sentences, checking for correct use of grammar (first conditional, for + -ing, because), vocabulary, and sentence clarity. They provide written feedback or corrections using a simple checklist (e.g., "Grammar OK?", "Vocabulary OK?", "Clear idea?"). Teacher monitors and provides support.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Importance of Water".</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Story-Writing and Summary. Students work in groups of 3-4 to perform two writing tasks:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Summarize the text:</b> First, each group writes a 3-5 sentence summary of the "Our Water, Our Future" text from Stage 2, focusing on its main points. They must include at least one "because" clause and one "for + -ing" structure. (e.g., "Water is an essential resource because we use it daily for drinking and cooking.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Collaborative Story:</b> Building on the summary, each group then collaboratively writes a short story (5-7 sentences) about a family or community's efforts to conserve water. The story should start with a challenge (e.g., a water shortage forecast or a drought) and describe how they act. Groups must incorporate at least two first conditional sentences (e.g., "If we collect rainwater, we will have water for plants."), and use at least five new vocabulary words (e.g., <i>drought, tap, conserve, limit, filter</i>). They should also discuss and propose solutions (sociolinguistic competence: "We could save water by turning off taps while washing hands."). One student acts as the scribe, but all contribute ideas and sentences.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher circulates, encouraging collaboration, providing vocabulary support, and prompting students to use the target grammar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist and a simple rubric for the group's written summary and collaborative story.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The written summary and collaborative story produced by each group in Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Rubric):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content (Summary & Story):</b> Clearly conveys main points of water importance and conservation efforts. (25%)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Correct use of First Conditional, "for + verb + ing", and "because" clauses. (30%)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates a variety of target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>shortage, conserve, essential, potable, daily</i>). (25%)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Cohesion:</b> Sentences are clear and ideas flow logically. (10%)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> All group members contribute to the written product (observed). (10%)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new thing I learned about writing about water conservation today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the summary or story was most challenging for me?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did working with my group help me write better sentences about water conservation?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific grammar point (e.g., 'if' clauses, 'because') do I need to practice more in my writing?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The collaborative story writing worked well to encourage students to use the target language communicatively. The peer check in Stage 3 was effective for immediate error correction. Some students struggled with sentence variety; more modeling of complex sentences might be needed. Adjustments: Next time, provide a sentence bank for the semi-controlled practice to offer more scaffolding for lower-proficiency students, and explicitly link pronunciation practice (e.g., of "forecast," "important") to how they write these words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on productive writing skills. In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their knowledge of water conservation and writing skills to mediate information, potentially by creating an informational poster or presenting their collaborative story to another group, requiring them to summarize and explain their written work.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>